## Secure Coding Practices

### Principles

```
1. INPUT VALIDATION
   ├─ Never trust user input
   ├─ Validate type, format, length, content
   ├─ Whitelist safe values, reject others
   ├─ Validate on server (never client-only)
   └─ Example: Email must match regex, max 256 chars

2. OUTPUT ENCODING
   ├─ Encode output based on context
   ├─ HTML context: Encode <, >, &, ", '
   ├─ JavaScript context: Different encoding
   ├─ URL context: URL encode
   └─ Database context: Use parameterized queries

3. PRINCIPLE OF LEAST PRIVILEGE
   ├─ Users: Minimum permissions needed
   ├─ Services: Run as unprivileged user
   ├─ Database: Use role-based access
   ├─ API: Limit scope of tokens
   └─ Example: Support staff shouldn't see salaries

4. DEFENSE IN DEPTH
   ├─ Multiple layers of security
   ├─ One layer compromised ≠ total failure
   ├─ Example:
   │  - Input validation (Layer 1)
   │  - Parameterized queries (Layer 2)
   │  - WAF blocking injections (Layer 3)
   │  - IDS detecting attacks (Layer 4)
   └─ Attacker must bypass all layers

5. FAIL SECURELY
   ├─ On error: Deny access (don't allow)
   ├─ Don't expose system info
   ├─ Log the error for debugging
   ├─ Return generic error to user
   └─ Example: "Login failed" not "Password incorrect"

6. USE SECURITY LIBRARIES
   ├─ Don't implement crypto yourself
   ├─ Use tested, vetted libraries
   ├─ Examples:
   │  - bcrypt for passwords
   │  - NaCl for encryption
   │  - OWASP ESAPI for validation
   └─ Regular updates of libraries

7. SECURE BY DEFAULT
   ├─ Secure settings enabled by default
   ├─ Must explicitly opt-in to insecure features
   ├─ Example: HTTPS enabled, not opt-in
   └─ Security shouldn't be configuration task
```

